; A `cond` with no `else` answers the integer 0 when no test fires -
; literally: `lowerConds` folds the clauses right to left around
; `(mkEInt 0)`. That is a real value at `Int` and at `Bool`, where 0
; is `false`. At every other type it is a null wearing whatever the
; signature claims.
;
; Nothing refused it. `if` has AX3004 and `match` has AX3005; `cond`
; had neither, which is the shape a reader has no way to predict -
; and `docs/reference.md`'s own `classify` example is an `else`-less
; `cond` returning `String`. Measured, before 2026-08-17:
;
;   (:: f (-> Int String))
;   (fn (f n) (cond ((> n 100) "big") ((> n 2) "mid")))
;   (strLen (f 0))    ->  `check` OK, exit 139, no output
;
; It gets AX3005 rather than a number of its own: the property is the
; one that code already names - the form can fall through - and one
; explanation covering both forms is honest where a second number
; would only record that `cond` was written later.
;
; The anchor is the first clause's TEST. The `cond` node itself has no
; span to offer - `parseCondClauses` builds it with `mkNode`, which
; stamps 0 - so a guard written against `(nodeSpan e)` can never fire,
; which is how the first version of this refusal passed every probe by
; doing nothing.
;
; Arrows, tuples, lists and pointers stay permitted, deliberately:
; none of them appears in an `else`-less `cond` anywhere in the tree,
; and widening a refusal on no evidence is how a checker acquires
; rules nobody can justify later.

; Refused: 0 is not a `String`.
(:: sizeName (-> Int String))
(fn (sizeName n)
  (cond
    ((> n 100) "big")
    ((> n 2) "mid")))

; Accepted: 0 is an `Int`, so this one really does have an answer.
(:: sizeRank (-> Int Int))
(fn (sizeRank n)
  (cond
    ((> n 100) 2)
    ((> n 2) 1)))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) (sizeRank 5))
